1. Association ("Uses-a")
Association is the most basic relationship where two classes interact with each other but remain entirely independent. There is no concept of ownership, and both objects have their own separate life cycles.

2. Aggregation ("Has-a")
Aggregation is a specialized, weak form of association where a parent class owns a child class, but they can still exist independently. If the parent object is destroyed, the child object survives.
In C#, this is typically achieved through Dependency Injection—passing an existing object into a constructor or setting it via a property.

3. Composition ("Part-of")
Composition is a strong form of aggregation with exclusive ownership. The child object cannot exist meaningfully without the parent. 
The parent class is fully responsible for managing the instantiation and destruction of the child object.
In C#, you implement composition by instantiating the child class directly inside the parent class (such as inside the constructor)

