In C#, a delegate is a type-safe object that references a method with a specific parameter list and return type. 
Essentially, it acts as a "function pointer" or a variable that holds a method instead of a value, allowing you to pass methods as arguments and invoke them dynamically.

Why Are Delegates Used?
Delegates are fundamental to modern C# development because they introduce flexibility and decouple your code:
1. Implementing Callbacks: You can pass a method into another method as a parameter. 
This is highly useful for long-running operations (like database connections or file downloads) where you want to notify the caller when the task completes.
2. Event-Driven Programming: Frameworks rely on delegates to handle user actions. 
For instance, a graphic button class doesn't know what code should run when clicked; it exposes an onClick event driven by a delegate so you can plugin your custom method later.
3. Pluggable & Dynamic Logic: They allow you to write highly reusable code. Instead of hardcoding a sorting routine, you can pass a custom comparison method via a delegate to a generic sorting function.
4. Multicasting: A single delegate can point to multiple methods at the same time. When the delegate is invoked, all assigned methods run sequentially.
