🔄 The Standard 2-Stage Deployment Workflow

Modern ADO deployments are configured using a single text file named azure-pipelines.yml. The workflow is split cleanly into a Build (CI) stage and a Deploy (CD) stage.

Stage 1: Build (CI)
• Trigger: A developer pushes code or merges a pull request into the main branch.
• Tasks: The build agent restores dependencies, compiles the application, runs tests, and compresses the compiled app into a .zip file or a Docker container image.
• Publishing: The resulting zip/container is saved back to ADO as an Artifact.

Stage 2: Deploy (CD)
• Trigger: Starts automatically as soon as Stage 1 finishes successfully.
• Tasks: The deploy agent downloads the artifact from the build stage, authenticates using the Service Connection, and pushes the package directly to the hosting server.


💻 Minimal YAML Pipeline Example (Deploying to Azure App Service)

Here is a standard, lightweight example of what an application deployment pipeline looks like:

trigger:
  - main # Runs whenever code merges into main

pool:
  vmImage: 'ubuntu-latest' # The OS of the agent running the job

stages:
- stage: Build
  displayName: 'Build and Package App'
  jobs:
  - job: BuildJob
    steps:
    # 1. Compile/Build your specific app code (e.g., Node, .NET, Python)
    - script: |
        npm install
        npm run build --if-present
      displayName: 'Install dependencies and build'

    # 2. Package the app files into a zip
    - task: ArchiveFiles@2
      inputs:
        rootFolderOrFile: '$(System.DefaultWorkingDirectory)'
        includeRootFolder: false
        archiveType: 'zip'
        archiveFile: '$(Build.ArtifactStagingDirectory)/app.zip'

    # 3. Save the zip in ADO so the deploy stage can grab it
    - task: PublishBuildArtifacts@1
      inputs:
        PathtoPublish: '$(Build.ArtifactStagingDirectory)/app.zip'
        ArtifactName: 'drop'

- stage: Deploy
  displayName: 'Deploy App to Production'
  dependsOn: Build
  condition: succeeded()
  jobs:
  - deployment: DeployJob
    environment: 'Production' # Links to ADO environments for tracking/approvals
    strategy:
      runOnce:
        deploy:
          steps:
          # 1. Pull the zip package created in the Build stage
          - download: current
            artifact: drop

          # 2. Securely push the package to your Azure Web App
          - task: AzureRmWebAppDeployment@4
            inputs:
              azureSubscription: 'My-Azure-Service-Connection' # Your preset connection name
              appType: 'webAppLinux'
              WebAppName: 'my-production-web-app'
              package: '$(Pipeline.Workspace)/drop/app.zip'


