Async/Await

using System;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

class Program
{
    // The Main method must be marked 'async Task' to use 'await' inside it
    static async Task Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Fetching data...");
        
        // Execution pauses here locally, freeing the thread, and resumes when done
        string data = await FetchDataFromApiAsync(); 
        
        Console.WriteLine($"Data received: {data.Length} characters.");
    }

    // 1. Suffix the method name with "Async" by convention
    // 2. Wrap the return type in a Task<T>
    static async Task<string> FetchDataFromApiAsync()
    {
        using HttpClient client = new HttpClient();
        
        // HttpClient exposes built-in asynchronous methods
        string result = await client.GetStringAsync("https://github.com");
        
        return result;
    }
}

When the C# compiler encounters the async keyword, it triggers a process called compiler lowering. 
It transforms your clean code into a highly optimized, hidden State Machine (struct) behind the scenes.
1. The Hit: Your code runs synchronously until it hits the first await statement.
2. The Yield: If the awaited task isn't finished yet, the state machine captures the method's local variables, pauses execution, and returns a Task object to the caller. 
The executing thread goes back to the thread pool to handle other work.
3. The Resume: When the external hardware or network driver signals that the job is complete, the thread pool assigns an available thread to re-enter the state machine and resume your code exactly where it left off.

