1. Concurrency in C# (Asynchronous & Non-Blocking)

Concurrency means multiple tasks make progress over overlapping time periods. In modern C#, this is overwhelmingly achieved using the async and await keywords.
Crucially, async/await does not necessarily spin up new threads. 
Instead, when a thread hits an await on an I/O operation (like downloading a file), the thread is released back to the system to do other work. 
When the file finishes downloading, a thread hooks back in to finish the task.

// Example: Concurrently fetching data from multiple APIs
public async Task FetchDashboardDataAsync()
{
    // Both requests start and make progress concurrently.
    // The current thread is NOT blocked; it can keep the UI responsive.
    Task<string> weatherTask = _client.GetStringAsync("https://weather.com");
    Task<string> stocksTask = _client.GetStringAsync("https://stocks.com");

    // Wait for both to finish without blocking CPU resources
    await Task.WhenAll(weatherTask, stocksTask); 

    string weather = weatherTask.Result;
    string stocks = stocksTask.Result;
}


2. Parallelism in C# (Split and Conquer)

Parallelism is a subset of concurrency. It splits a massive chunk of data or CPU-heavy work into smaller pieces and throws them across multiple CPU cores at the exact same moment.
C# achieves this via the Task Parallel Library (TPL) and PLINQ. These tools use the .NET Thread Pool to scale threads up to match your hardware's capacity.

// Example: Parallel processing of independent data
public void BlurImages(List<Image> images)
{
    // The TPL automatically handles partitioning the list
    // and processing them simultaneously across your CPU cores.
    Parallel.ForEach(images, image => 
    {
        ApplyHeavyBlurFilter(image); // CPU-intensive calculation
    });
}

• Use Concurrency (async/await) when: You are waiting on something external to your code (calling a web API, reading a database, opening a file). 
Keeping the application from freezing is your main priority.
• Use Parallelism (Parallel / PLINQ) when: You have a massive array, list, or loops doing heavy mathematical or data operations that don't depend on each other. 
Maximizing your raw hardware power is your main priority.
