ANGULAR
1) OnPush - The ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush strategy instructs Angular to skip checking a component unless specific, explicit triggers occur. 
By default, Angular checks every component on every asynchronous event (clicks, timers, HTTP calls). OnPush optimizes performance by limiting checks to a strict subset of events.

An OnPush component will only re-render under four conditions:
1. An @Input() property reference changes (primitive change or object reference swap).
2. An Event originates from the component or its immediate children (e.g., a template click handler).
3. An Observable bound via the AsyncPipe emits a new value.
4. A Signal value used in the template updates

2) Promise vs Observables

3) SwitchMap, mergeMap, concatMap, ExhaustMap
- In Angular and RxJS, switchMap, mergeMap, and concatMap are higher-order mapping operators. They take a value from a source Observable, map it to a new inner Observable (like an HTTP request), 
and flatten the result so you receive the raw data instead of an Observable of Observables.
The fundamental difference lies in how they handle multiple inner Observables when the source emits values rapidly

4) Interceptors and route guards
- Angular HTTP Interceptors act as middleware that sits between your application and the backend server. 
They allow you to intercept, inspect, and transform outgoing HTTP requests and incoming responses globally.

Core Use Cases
• Authentication: Automatically attach a Bearer JWT token to every outgoing request.
• Global Error Handling: Catch failed requests (like 401 Unauthorized or 500 Server Error) in one central location.
• Loading Spinners: Trigger a UI spinner when network requests start and hide it when they complete.
• Logging & Analytics: Measure server response times and log API activity.

5) Reactive forms vs template driven forms
-Template-driven forms rely heavily on directives placed directly inside the HTML template.
• How it works: Uses Angular's two-way data-binding directive ([(ngModel)]) to manage the form instance.
• Setup: Import FormsModule into your module.
• Best used for: Simple use cases like a login box, search bar, or email sign-up form where logic is minimal.
• Pros: Quick to set up; requires very little TypeScript code.
• Cons: Harder to unit test; difficult to scale or handle complex dynamic validation rules.

- Reactive forms use a model-driven approach where controls are explicitly declared and managed inside the component's TypeScript class.
• How it works: Form controls (FormControl, FormGroup, FormArray) are instantiated programmatically in code and linked to the template.
• Setup: Import ReactiveFormsModule into your module.
• Best used for: Complex enterprise apps, multi-step wizards, dynamic fields, and custom or asynchronous validators.
• Pros: Highly testable without rendering the DOM; synchronous data flow; predictable and scalable structure.
• Cons: Requires writing more boilerplate code in the TypeScript file.

6) How to manage shared state ?
- Option 1: Services + Signals (Recommended for Modern Angular)
Using Angular Signals inside a shared service is the cleanest, most performant way to handle state. 
It eliminates unnecessary change detection passes and provides a simple API without manual subscription management.
